    private function section($content, $type, $title, $body, $media, $order): void
    {
        $content->sections()->create(['type' => $type, 'title' => $title, 'body' => $body, 'media_id' => $media, 'sort_order' => $order, 'enabled' => true]);
    }

    private function articles(): array
    {
        return [
            ['preparing-a-packaging-brief', 'Before the design: build a clearer packaging brief.', 'The most useful packaging conversations begin with the product and its journey, not a finished mock-up.', 'materials', '<p><em>Illustrative editorial guide. Product suitability and project details require individual review.</em></p><h2>Start with what the package needs to do</h2><p>A useful brief describes the product before describing the pack. Is the product dry, liquid, granular, or sensitive to a particular environment? How much is filled into each pack? How is it stored, transported, opened, and used? These questions create the context for format and material discussions.</p><h2>Describe the journey</h2><p>Write down the expected filling process, transport conditions, storage environment, and customer handling. A pack that spends most of its life in a warehouse can face different practical questions from one repeatedly handled on a retail shelf. Avoid treating a competitor sample as a complete technical specification.</p><h2>Bring dimensions and quantities together</h2><p>Approximate dimensions, fill volume or weight, target quantities, and the available space for artwork are useful starting points. If a sample exists, record what you like about it and what should change. Separate a firm requirement from a preference that can be explored.</p><h2>Make room for print information</h2><p>List the text, symbols, barcode requirements, and artwork elements that need to appear. Identify who will supply approved artwork and who will confirm its accuracy. Packaging design and label content often depend on product-specific rules; those rules should be checked by the responsible business team.</p><h2>Close the brief with open questions</h2><p>A strong brief does not pretend every answer is known. It makes uncertainties visible: material compatibility, closure preferences, dimensions still under review, and quantities that may change. This helps a technical and commercial discussion focus on the decisions that matter.</p><p>Use the Amalez Pack Trade contact form to share your requirement. The formats shown on this website are illustrative and do not establish manufacturing capability or product availability.</p>'],
            ['choosing-flexible-packaging-formats', 'Stand-up, spout, or roll: ask the format questions first.', 'A practical way to compare flexible packaging concepts without assuming that one format fits every product.', 'pouches', '<p><em>Illustrative guide to packaging terminology, not a product-specific recommendation.</em></p><h2>Compare use, not just appearance</h2><p>Flexible packaging covers a range of structures and formats. A visually appealing pouch is only part of the decision. Filling method, product behavior, protection requirements, handling, and the intended opening experience all influence the brief.</p><h2>Stand-up pouch questions</h2><p>For a freestanding pouch concept, discuss filled dimensions, stability, available display area, and how the pack will be opened. If resealing matters, identify the expected pattern of use rather than simply specifying a zipper. The material structure and closure need review for the actual product.</p><h2>Spout pouch questions</h2><p>For a pourable product concept, useful starting points include viscosity, spout position, closure preferences, filling temperature, and the filling process. A spout does not by itself establish compatibility with food, chemicals, or a particular shelf-life requirement. Those considerations need specific assessment.</p><h2>Roll-stock questions</h2><p>When discussing packaging supplied in rolls, bring the filling-line requirements into the conversation early. Web width, repeat length, core size, winding direction, sealing conditions, and artwork orientation can all be relevant. The equipment requirements should be confirmed by the responsible operator.</p><h2>Keep the decision grounded</h2><p>Record the product, the expected journey, and the key uncertainties. Comparing formats against that brief is more useful than choosing solely from a photograph. Materials and structures should be evaluated for the particular application before commercial commitments are made.</p><p>The service guides on this website are illustrative concepts for discussion. Amalez Pack Trade does not publish unverified certifications, machinery ownership, or manufacturing claims.</p>'],
            ['artwork-and-label-handover', 'A cleaner artwork handover starts with clearer information.', 'Organize dimensions, label content, and approval responsibilities before discussing printed packaging.', 'labels', '<p><em>General editorial guidance for packaging and printing conversations.</em></p><h2>Establish the working dimensions</h2><p>Before refining visual details, identify the intended format and the dimensions that govern the artwork. Keep approved dimensions distinct from estimates. If a dieline or artwork template is available, confirm its version and the project it belongs to.</p><h2>Separate content from presentation</h2><p>Product names, ingredients or other regulated information, instructions, barcodes, and commercial text should be approved by the responsible business team. A polished design file cannot resolve inaccurate source information. Maintain a clear record of which content has been approved and which details remain open.</p><h2>Identify the files and their purpose</h2><p>Label files clearly and record the intended use of each version. A reference image, an editable design file, and a final approved production file are different things. Make sure everyone involved understands which file is being discussed.</p><h2>Describe the intended result</h2><p>Explain the desired appearance and any practical concerns, including readability, surface conditions, handling, or exposure. Printing and finishing options should be discussed in the context of the material and application. Avoid assuming an effect shown in a mock-up is automatically available or suitable.</p><h2>Agree on the approval path</h2><p>List who confirms dimensions, who owns the product information, and who approves the final artwork. A simple checklist can make a handover more transparent: correct version, approved content, reviewed dimensions, and clearly identified outstanding questions.</p><p>Use the contact page to describe your labels or printed-packaging requirement. Final technical and commercial details require confirmation; this article does not claim specific equipment or production capabilities.</p>'],
        ];
    }
}
